  "query_preview": "..A cold front has pushed into NW Arizona early this\nmorning and will continue eastward through the day. Some rain and\nsnow showers have developed along the front, mainly affecting\nnorthern Coconino County, though some additional light showers may\ndevelop as far south as the Flagstaff/Williams area a bit later \n(4-8 am). An ADOT cam at Jacob Lake (8000 ft) shows some light \nsnow accumulation on the roadway, and around 1-3\" of snow is \nforecast for the Kaibab Plateau through this morning. Elsewhere, \nany accumulation will be spotty and brief. Southwest winds \nremained elevated overnight, especially just downwind of terrain \nfeatures where some gusts of 30-40 mph have been observed. Winds \nwill pick up for most places again later this morning and continue\nthrough the afternoon, gusting 25-35 mph. Later tonight into \nSaturday morning, a trailing shortwave rotates through northern \nArizona, bringing a brief period of large scale lift. Enough \nmoisture is forecast to linger mainly along/east of I-17/US89 for \nisolated to scattered snow showers tonight into early Saturday \nmorning. Any snowfall accumulations should remain light (0.5\" or \nless) and favor the higher terrain of eastern Arizona. The weekend\nis looking fair but cool, with relatively light winds. Next week \n- a couple of troughs are forecast to move eastward across the \nregion, one on Monday and another Wednesday or Thursday. There \nremains some uncertainty in strength/track of these systems, but \nthey appear minor overall. Precipitation chances in our latest \nforecasts for these days are mostly 10% or less.",
